{"t":1794447093,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"156433d0-b17e-46c3-94bd-baf5a0053784","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-11T23:51:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 112351\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n651 PM EST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo significant changes to the current forecast package. Latest model \nruns continue to trend drier in regards to precipitation chances \nThursday through Saturday AM. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) First accumulating snow for parts of the Alleghenies with\n  rain showers and brisk conditions elsewhere, Thursday through\n  Saturday.\n\n- 2) Cooler temperatures & dry condtions expected for much of\n  next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...First accumulating snow for parts of the Alleghenies with rain \nshowers and brisk conditions elsewhere, Thursday through Saturday.\n\nNo significant changes are expected to the current forecast package \noutside timing of precipitation chances Thursday into Friday. Still \nmonitoring an area of low pressure ejecting out of the southern \nGreat Lakes/Ohio River Valley and it's associated cold frontal \nboundary that is set to cross late Thursday morning into Thursday \nafternoon. The low will shift off the Delmarva coast Thursday \nevening into Thursday night before pulling away Friday into the \nweekend. A secondary front and piece of shortwave energy will pivot \nthrough the area Friday into Saturday morning reinforcing the colder \nair along with upslope precipitation over the mountains. \n\nAfter a prolonged stretch of dry weather the chance for widespread \nprecipitation returns. Unfortunately, this will not be a drought \nbuster by any means with QPF values trending downward, especially \neast of the Blue Ridge per the latest model guidance. Most locations \nwill see a tenth of an inch or less in the precipitation department \nThursday through Saturday. Higher amounts of precipitation will be \nnoted over northeast MD and the Catoctins where a tenth to a quarter \nof an inch of precipitation are expected. The highest precipitation \ntotals will be in the upslope areas of the Alleghenies with \nwidespread amounts of a quarter to a half an inch expected. Most of \nthis will come in the form of snow late Thursday night into Friday \nas colder air wraps in. \n\nSpeaking of snow, this will be the first flakes and accumulation of \nthe season for portions of the Allegheny Mountains. Accumulations \nwill likely remain just shy of advisory criteria due largely in part \nto marginal temperatures at the onset and during the during the \nduration of the event. Most locations will see a coating-2\" above \n2000 feet with 2-3\" at elevations above 3500 feet. Locally higher \ntotals are possible along Backbone Mountain at Hoyes Crest and down \ntoward Spruce Knob pending the orientation of any streamer activity \noff of Lake Erie/Lake Michigan Friday night into Saturday morning. \nThis will be the time window of greatest impact on high elevation \nroadways given temperatures below freezing. Roadways impacted \nincluded US-219 from Keysers Ridge south into WV, US-48 west of \nBismarck/Mt. Storm, WV, US-33 west of Franklin, WV, and US-250 west \nof Monterey, VA. Expect temperatures in the 20s leading to fluffier \nsnow characters with ratios of 13:1 or better. \n\nHigh temperatures Thursday will range from the upper 40s over the \nmountains to low 60s near the bay. Temperatures tumble Thursday \nevening with lows Thursday night in the 30s and 40s. Winds crank up \nout of the west and northwest Thursday afternoon and evening \ncontinuing through Friday with gusts between 20-30 mph. Locally \nhigher gusts are possible along the ridges. These winds will yield \nwind chills in the 30s and 40s for both Friday and Saturday with air \ntemperatures outside the mountains struggling to get out of the \nupper 40s and low 50s in most locations. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler temperatures & dry condtions expected \nfor much of next week. \n\nAs the weekend's longwave trough slowly pushes offshore early next \nweek, winds could remain elevated through Sunday before diminishing \nbetween Monday & Tuesday in the wake of the cold front. Temperatures \nare likely to remain much cooler areawide throughout the week, with \nmany areas seeing near or below freezing temperatures overnight \nbeginning this weekend through the remainder of the forecast period.\n\nOverall, generally calm, clear conditions persist throughout the \nweek as a upper-level ridging transitions overhead. Daytime \ntemperatures moderate a bit later in the week with continued high \npressure, with afternoon highs in the mid-to-upper 50s on\nWednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nVFR conditions will continue this evening with light winds out of \nthe south and southwest at less than 10 kts. Mid and high level \ncloud cover will continue to increase as a wave of low pressure and \ncold front eject east from the southern Great Lakes/Ohio River \nValley. This system will bring pockets of light rain to the \nterminals on and off throughout Thursday. Shower activity should \npush into terminals west of MRB prior 13z/8am before advancing east \ntoward the corridor late Thursday morning into Thursday afternoon. \nActivity will be scattered and light in nature leading to more in \nthe way of cig reductions compared to vsbys. \n\nWind will become the bigger issue for aviation Thursday afternoon \nand continuing through Saturday. Expect gusts on the order of 20-30 \nkts out of the northwest with locally higher gusts over the ridges \nand waters. Winds will be reinforced out of the northwest Friday \ninto Saturday as another ripple of energy pulses through. A spotty \nsprinkle is possible east of the mountains Friday although most \nterminals outside of those west of KCBE will be dry. VFR conditions \nare expected Friday into the weekend outside of the mountains where \nupslope snow showers will persists. \n\nVFR conditions are expected to continue into early next week. Winds \ndiminish Sunday into Monday while shifting northerly Tuesday into \nmidweek as high pressure settles over the region. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSub-SCA level winds are expected to continue overnight ahead of an \napproaching cold frontal boundary. SCA conditions are likely \nThursday afternoon through Saturday as a wave of low pressure and \nseries of fronts cross the waters. Expect north to northwesterly \nwinds to gusts 20-30 kts at times Thursday evening and throughout \nFriday into Saturday.  Winds likely start to diminish back below SCA \nlevels Sunday and Monday, shifting more northerly by Tuesday. Sub-\nSCA level conditions are expected with high pressure for the middle \nof the next week. \n\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nAction Stages are likely Thursday night before winds shift to \nnorthwest behind a cold front. A strong frontal passage will \nbring a prolonged period of blowout tides Friday and through \nearly next week.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...AVS/EST/SRT\nAVIATION...EST/SRT\nMARINE...EST/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1794447040,"days":{"2026-11-11":{"hi":null,"lo":49,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-11-12":{"hi":64,"lo":43,"pop":42,"short":"Chance Light Rain"},"2026-11-13":{"hi":51,"lo":36,"pop":24,"short":"Slight Chance Light Rain then Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-14":{"hi":51,"lo":37,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-15":{"hi":53,"lo":36,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-16":{"hi":53,"lo":35,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-17":{"hi":54,"lo":34,"pop":2,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-18":{"hi":57,"lo":null,"pop":2,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1794445200,"n":48,"ceil":[25000,25000,16300,21600,null,null,null,null,null,null